# Copy to .env and fill in your values before running locally.
# All variables are required unless marked optional.

# ─── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DB_USER=minicrm
DB_PASSWORD=changeme
DB_NAME=minicrm
DB_HOST=localhost
DB_PORT=5432
# Maximum database connections in the pool (default: 10).
# Should not exceed PostgreSQL's max_connections minus connections reserved for other services.
# DB_POOL_MAX=10

# ─── Coverage/TIA Database (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Coverage/TIA data (coverage_units, coverage_sessions, etc.) lives in its own
# database, separate from the product DB above — see server/src/coverageDb.ts.
# All COVERAGE_DB_* vars fall back to the product DB's own credentials/host/port
# above when unset (both databases live on the same Postgres instance in every
# environment this repo currently targets); only COVERAGE_DB_NAME has its own
# default ('minicrm_coverage') rather than inheriting DB_NAME, so a misconfigured
# environment can never accidentally point this pool at the product database.
# COVERAGE_DB_USER=minicrm
# COVERAGE_DB_PASSWORD=changeme
COVERAGE_DB_NAME=minicrm_coverage
# COVERAGE_DB_HOST=localhost
# COVERAGE_DB_PORT=5432
# COVERAGE_DB_POOL_MAX=10

# All of the following are OPTIONAL — docker-compose.yml supplies `${VAR:-false}` defaults and
# every server read is `=== 'true'`, so unset and false behave identically. Uncomment
# only if you need them on your DEV server; the test stack sets its own values in
# docker-compose.test.yml and never reads these.
#
# Register the coverage/TIA control API and session-management routes at boot
# (MINCRM-663). COVERAGE_INSTRUMENTATION also gates whether routes/coverage.ts registers
# at all — see server/src/server.ts. Production must never set either.
# COVERAGE_INSTRUMENTATION=true
# COVERAGE_SESSION_MANAGEMENT=true
# Register the coverage/TIA query and ingestion routers at boot (MINCRM-685), the same
# way the two above work. Once MINCRM-685's route-layer change and corrective migration
# land, these three replace the coverage_mapping_query, coverage_reporting_query, and
# coverage_pipeline_ingestion feature_flags rows — internal CI/dev test infrastructure
# has no business being toggleable from the product's own admin Settings page. Unset
# will then mean the routes are never registered, so every path under them 404s rather
# than 403ing. The coverage-dashboard app needs MAPPING and REPORTING; TIA record-mode
# ingestion needs PIPELINE. Production must never set any of them.
# COVERAGE_MAPPING_QUERY=true
# COVERAGE_REPORTING_QUERY=true
# COVERAGE_PIPELINE_INGESTION=true
# Lets the standalone coverage-dashboard app (coverage-dashboard/, see its own README)
# query coverage/gap-analysis reporting with no CRM admin login. It is an internal
# engineering tool with no auth system of its own; this drops the auth chain for only
# the three routers that app calls (reporting, sessions, mapping), not the ingest/
# management side of the coverage/TIA API. It does NOT disable those routes' feature
# flags — those still apply org-wide (MINCRM-694). Once MINCRM-685's route-layer change
# lands, reporting and mapping must ALSO be registered via their own env vars above or
# they 404 regardless of this setting. The server guards all of this behind
# NODE_ENV !== 'production'.
# COVERAGE_DASHBOARD_NO_AUTH=true
# Disables the login rate limiter on THIS (dev) server so suites can log in repeatedly.
# The test stack hardcodes its own E2E=true and does not read this value.
# E2E=true

# When 'true', every coverage route's access check switches from a bare
# admin-role check to the coverage:admin capability (MINCRM-637). Unset
# (default) preserves today's exact requireRole('admin') behavior — this
# flag exists to verify capability-based access against real role-assignment
# data before the role-based fallback is removed in a follow-up. NOT purely a
# narrowing: it also WIDENS access for a bearer-authenticated service_account
# user holding coverage:admin via a custom role, which requireRole('admin')
# can never grant (service_account never satisfies role === 'admin'). Verify
# no service_account unexpectedly holds coverage:admin before enabling this
# in a security-sensitive environment — see docs/dev/coverage.md's "Access
# Control" section.
# COVERAGE_CAPABILITY_GATING=true

# ─── Coverage/TIA Policy Configuration (optional, MINCRM-637) ─────────────────
# Full env-var surface resolved by server/src/coverageAgent/coveragePolicyConfig.ts.
# See docs/dev/coverage.md's "Policy Configuration" section for defaults and rationale.
# COVERAGE_INSTRUMENTATION, COVERAGE_SESSION_MANAGEMENT, COVERAGE_MAPPING_QUERY,
# COVERAGE_REPORTING_QUERY, and COVERAGE_PIPELINE_INGESTION are declared in the
# Coverage/TIA Database section above.
# V8 coverage detail level: 'block' (default) or 'function'.
# COVERAGE_GRANULARITY=block
# Days a coverage_units/coverage_test_links row survives untouched before
# the daily retention-pruning cron removes it. Default: 30.
# COVERAGE_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# Safety-net confidence floor for test selection — a matched unit below this
# score is treated as untrustworthy. Default: 0.3.
# TIA_MIN_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD=0.3
# Safety-net unmapped-changed-unit ratio ceiling before targeted selection
# is distrusted and falls back to the full suite. Default: 0.5.
# TIA_MAX_UNMAPPED_RATIO=0.5

# ─── Auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED: Must be a cryptographically random string, minimum 32 characters.
# Generate with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# WARNING: Never use 'changeme' or any short/guessable value in any environment.
#          Using a weak secret allows attackers to forge valid JWTs for any role.
JWT_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_RANDOM_64_CHAR_HEX_STRING

# ─── Server ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3001

# Comma-separated list of allowed CORS origins. Defaults to the nginx client port (80).
# Set to your deployed frontend URL in staging/production (e.g. https://app.example.com).
#
# LAN access (mobile devices on the same network):
#   When a device connects via the server's LAN IP (e.g. http://192.168.1.100),
#   the browser sends that IP as the CORS origin. Add it to this list alongside
#   localhost to allow LAN access without opening a wildcard.
#   Example: CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost,http://192.168.1.100
#
# Never use '*' here — credentials (cookies) require explicit origins.
#
# Standalone coverage-dashboard dev server (MINCRM-628/629):
#   Running coverage-dashboard's own dev server (port 5174) alongside the main
#   client (port 5173) needs 5174 added here too. With COVERAGE_DASHBOARD_NO_AUTH
#   unset (below), it authenticates via the same session cookie as the CRM
#   client, so its origin must be explicitly allowlisted for withCredentials
#   requests to succeed.
#   Example: CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost,http://localhost:5173,http://localhost:5174
#
# Custom hostname (optional):
#   Mapping a friendly name in /etc/hosts (e.g. `127.0.0.1 dev.minicrm.local`) and
#   opening http://dev.minicrm.local:5173 gives a memorable dev URL with full HMR,
#   without needing a privileged port. The origin must be listed here or the server
#   rejects every request — app.ts allowlists origins explicitly, with no wildcard.
#   Example: CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173,http://dev.minicrm.local:5173
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost

# ─── Admin seed user (created on first boot if no admin exists) ───────────────
ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
ADMIN_NAME=Admin
# REQUIRED: Must meet the application password policy (8+ chars, 1 letter, 1 number).
# WARNING: Do not use 'changeme' — this admin account is created on first boot.
ADMIN_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_STRONG_PASSWORD

# ─── File storage encryption ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED if using file attachments (S3 or MinIO storage).
# Must be a 64-character hex string (32 bytes) used for AES-256-GCM encryption
# of the storage secret access key at rest. Without this, configuring storage
# via the Admin Settings UI will throw an error.
# Generate with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
NODE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_RANDOM_64_CHAR_HEX_STRING

# ─── Email (SMTP) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional in development (emails are logged to stdout instead of delivered).
# Required in production for overdue task digest emails and assignment notifications.
# Without SMTP_HOST, emails are silently skipped — no error is shown to users.

SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=notifications@example.com
SMTP_PASS=REPLACE_WITH_SMTP_PASSWORD
SMTP_FROM=MiniCRM <notifications@example.com>

# DKIM acknowledgement (optional but recommended for production).
# The server logs a startup warning when SMTP_FROM is set but this variable is absent,
# because missing DKIM causes outbound mail to land in spam.
# Set to "relay-managed" when your SMTP relay (SendGrid, SES, Mailgun) handles DKIM signing.
# Set to "postfix-managed" when an MTA on the same host signs outbound messages.
# See docs/operations.md#email-deliverability for full setup instructions.
# SMTP_DKIM_PRIVATE_KEY=relay-managed

# Base URL of the client application, used to generate links in email bodies.
# Set to the URL users access the app from (e.g. https://crm.example.com).
APP_URL=http://localhost

# Base URL the SSO callback redirects to after a successful login. (MINCRM-399)
# Set to the Vite dev server in development, or the public app URL in production.
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5173
# SSO callback URL base — must point to the Express API server, not the frontend. (MINCRM-399)
# IdP will POST/redirect to SSO_CALLBACK_BASE_URL/api/v1/auth/sso/callback.
SSO_CALLBACK_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001

# ─── Automated database backups (optional) ────────────────────────────────────
# Only used when the backup service is enabled via the "backup" Docker Compose profile.
# See docs/operations.md for enable instructions.
#
# BACKUP_SCHEDULE — cron expression or @daily / @hourly shorthand (default: @daily).
# BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS — number of daily backups to keep before rotating (default: 7).
# BACKUP_SCHEDULE=@daily
# BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=7

# ─── Error tracking (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set SENTRY_DSN to enable server-side error reporting to Sentry.
# Set VITE_SENTRY_DSN to enable client-side error reporting to Sentry.
# Both are optional — omit to disable Sentry entirely (e.g. local development).
# Never set these in test environments; the integration is automatically skipped
# when NODE_ENV=test (server) or Vitest mode (client).
# SENTRY_DSN=https://examplePublicKey@o0.ingest.sentry.io/0
# VITE_SENTRY_DSN=https://examplePublicKey@o0.ingest.sentry.io/0
